<description>
Move every object above the solid bottom stripe upward by its own pixel count.

Input structure
The grid has a dominant background, exactly one full-width non-background row
that acts as a fixed stripe, and one or more 4-connected monochrome objects
above that stripe.

Rule
Clear each object from its original cells, then translate it straight upward
by the number of pixels in that object, preserving its shape and color. Leave
the full-width stripe unchanged.

Key insight
Each object's area is also its vertical displacement; the full-width row is a
fixed baseline, not a moving object.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "each 4-connected monochrome foreground object s above the full-width bottom stripe",
  "path": "straight upward by s.area rows",
  "trajectory": "move only s to the endpoint, preserving its shape and color",
  "transform": "clear s.old cells to background; keep the bottom stripe unchanged"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 8x8 [observed]
  background: 7
  stripe:
    position: last row (row h-1), full-width, single solid color
    color: 8 [observed]; any non-bg color works per solver
  objects:
    count: 2-4 monochrome 4-connected components above the stripe
    colors: each object has a distinct color, different from bg and stripe color
    placement: every object rests with its bottom edge on row h-2 (directly above stripe)
    column_disjoint: no two objects share any column (guarantees no post-move overlap)
    column_gap: >=1 empty column between adjacent objects
    height: 1-3 rows
    area: 1-6 pixels; constrained by area <= (h-2) - (height-1) so displaced shape stays on grid
    shape: arbitrary 4-connected within bbox; no symmetry required
  empty_space: all cells above the topmost object pixel are background
  rule: each object moves straight up by exactly its area (pixel count); stripe unchanged

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Create 8x8 grid filled with bg=7. Place stripe (color 8) on last row.
    2. Choose N=2-4 objects. For each, pick a distinct color from {1-6,9}.
    3. Partition columns 0-7 into N non-overlapping spans with >=1 gap between each.
    4. For each object, pick area and generate a random 4-connected shape fitting
       within its column span and height<=3. Verify area <= 6 and area + top_row_of_shape <= h-2
       (so the output placement starting at row 0+ is valid).
    5. Place each object in the OUTPUT at its final (moved-up) position, i.e., with
       its bottom at row (h-2 - area). Verify no overlap and top >= 0.
    6. Derive INPUT by shifting each object back DOWN by its area so bottom = row h-2.
```
